.\n\nWait a second, let me verify each step again to make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors. Starting with h = 505. Then j = 505 - 29. 505 minus 29: 505 - 30 is 475, but since it's minus 29, that's 475 + 1 = 476. Yep, that checks out.\n\nThen d = j + 18. 476 + 18. Let's see, 476 + 20 is 496, but subtract 2 to get back to 18, so 496 - 2 = 494. Correct. Then x = 494 + 61. 494 + 60 is 554, plus 1 is 555. Hmm, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add them all up in sequence to see if there's another way to approach it. Starting from h = 505. Then j = h - 29, so 505 - 29 = 476. Then d = j + 18 = 476 + 18 = 494. Then x = d + 61 = 494 + 61 = 555. All the steps add up correctly.\n\nJust to double-check, maybe I can combine the equations to express x in terms of h directly. Let's see. Starting with:\n\nj = h - 29\n\nd = j + 18 = (h - 29) + 18 = h - 11\n\nThen x = d + 61 = (h - 11) + 61 = h + 50\n\nSince h is 505, then x = 505 + 50 = 555. Oh, that's a quicker way. Instead of calculating each variable step by step, I can substitute each subsequent variable into the next equation. So x ends up being h + 50. Since h is 505, 505 + 50 is definitely 555. That confirms the answer again.\n\nTherefore, I'm pretty confident that the value of x is 555. All the different methods I tried\u2014step-by-step calculation and algebraic substitution\u2014lead to the same result. I don't think I made any mistakes here, but it's good that I checked it multiple ways. Sometimes even if you do the steps right, arithmetic errors can creep in, but in this case, everything lines up.\n\n\n\nThe value of \\( x \\) is calculated as follows:\n\n1. Start with \\( h = 505 \\).\n2. Compute \\( j = h - 29 = 505 - 29 = 476 \\).\n3. Compute \\( d = j + 18 = 476 + 18 = 494 \\).\n4. Finally, \\( x = d + 61 = 494 + 61 = 555 \\).\n\nAlternatively, combining the equations:\n\\[\nx = (h - 29 + 18) + 61 = h + 50 = 505 + 50 = 555\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** 555"}
