t, which would be 453 - 40 = 413, and then subtract 2 more: 413 - 2 = 411. So i is 411?\n\nWait a second, that seems like i is equal to p. Because p was u + 19, which was 411, and then w was p + 42, which is 453, and then i is w - 42, bringing it back down to 411. So i is the same as p? That's interesting. Let me check again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting over:\n\nu = 392\n\np = u + 19 = 392 + 19. Let's verify 392 + 10 is 402, plus 9 is 411. Correct.\n\nw = p + 42 = 411 + 42. 411 + 40 is 451, plus 2 is 453. Correct.\n\ni = w - 42 = 453 - 42. 453 - 40 is 413, minus 2 is 411. Yep, that's right. So i ends up being 411, which is the same as p. So the answer should be 411. Let me just confirm once more to be absolutely sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different way to look at it algebraically without plugging in the numbers. Let's see:\n\nStarting with i = w - 42.\n\nBut w is p + 42, so substituting that in, i = (p + 42) - 42 = p. So i equals p. Since p is u + 19, then i is also u + 19. Since u is 392, i is 392 + 19 = 411. That's the same result. So even algebraically, it checks out. Therefore, i is 411. I think that's solid. No mistakes here.\n\n\n\nThe value of \\( i \\) is calculated as follows:\n\n1. Start with the given value of \\( u \\):\n   \\[\n   u = 392\n   \\]\n2. Calculate \\( p \\) by adding 19 to \\( u \\):\n   \\[\n   p = u + 19 = 392 + 19 = 411\n   \\]\n3. Determine \\( w \\) by adding 42 to \\( p \\):\n   \\[\n   w = p + 42 = 411 + 42 = 453\n   \\]\n4. Finally, find \\( i \\) by subtracting 42 from \\( w \\):\n   \\[\n   i = w - 42 = 453 - 42 = 411\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( i \\) is \\(\\boxed{411}\\)."}
